// Reading a line the user types, and turning it into a number that can be
// worked with, including both ways that can fail.
//
// Input needs somewhere to put the text before it exists as a value. In Jux
// that is an ordinary `String`: Rust's `read_line` appends to it, and the heap
// behind it is the global one, so no allocator has to be passed in.

// The standard library is Rust's. `stdin()` and `stdout()` are the free
// functions from `std::io`, imported by their Rust names.
import rust.std.stdin;
import rust.std.stdout;

// The constant, at the precision a double can hold. The Math lesson explains
// why it is declared here rather than imported.
const double Pi = 3.141592653589793;

// `print` always ends the line, and a question should leave the cursor where
// the answer is typed. Writing the bytes straight to standard output and
// flushing it does that; without the flush the text could sit in the buffer
// until after the program had already stopped to read.
void prompt(String text) {
    var out = stdout();
    out.write_all(text.as_bytes());
    out.flush();
}

// `int main` rather than `void main`, because the exit status is part of the
// answer: zero when a circle was measured, one when the input was unusable.
int main() {
    prompt("Circle radius: ");

    // The first failure: there was no line to read. `read_line` returns how
    // many bytes it added, and zero means the input ended with nothing before
    // it, which is different from an empty line (that still has its "\n").
    var line = "";
    if (stdin().read_line(line) == 0) {
        print("Nothing was entered");
        return 1;
    }

    // The second failure: there was a line, but it does not spell a number.
    // Rust's `parse` returns a `Result`, and a Rust `Result` reaches Jux as a
    // method that `throws` (Bindgen §G.5.4), so the failure is a `catch`.
    // `trim()` drops the line break and any stray spaces around the digits.
    double radius = 0.0;
    try {
        radius = line.trim().parse<double>();
    } catch (Exception e) {
        print("That is not a number");
        return 1;
    }

    // `toFixed(4)` is the Jux spelling of a four-digit precision; there are no
    // placeholder specs inside an interpolation.
    print($"Circumference: ${(2.0 * Pi * radius).toFixed(4)}");
    print($"Area:          ${(Pi * radius * radius).toFixed(4)}");

    // Nothing to release: the String's buffer goes back to the heap when
    // `line` goes out of scope.
    return 0;
}
